  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The six days from September 3 through September 6 contained no diplomatic communication from a named Iranian official announcing readiness for mediated ceasefire talks. Rather than opening a negotiating channel, the period produced the conflict's sharpest bilateral maritime exchange since June: on September 5, US forces struck three Iranian oil tankers in the Strait, permanently disabling two; the IRGC responded with direct missile fire at US warships in the same zone and issued a statement from General Rezaei promising more severe attacks if the American blockade continued. President Pezeshkian's September 1 conditional statement at the SCO summit in Bishkek — that Iran would return to the June MOU "if the US carries out its commitments" — was not transmitted through a named mediator as an offer of new talks, and followed within hours by mutual exchange of fire. The F2 resolution criteria — a named official or MFA formally announcing readiness for mediated ceasefire talks before EOD September 6 — was not met. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Between September 3 and September 17, no Iran-US indirect negotiating session with named senior principals and named mediators took place. On September 10, Trump told reporters talks were "possible within days" — a forward-looking media statement, not a description of an ongoing session. Pakistan and Qatar relayed messages through the period but no bilateral sitting occurred. The first qualifying session came on September 23 at the UN General Assembly margins in New York: Araghchi and US envoys Witkoff and Kushner occupied separate meeting rooms while Qatari, Pakistani, and Egyptian mediators shuttled between them — a confirmed indirect session with named senior officials from both sides, occurring six days after the F3 horizon. That September 23 session produced Iran's September 24 written road map for a 60-day regionwide ceasefire, phased Hormuz reopening, end to the US blockade, halt to attacks on Arab neighbors, and an agreed nuclear negotiation timeline — the most substantive diplomatic document of the conflict's third phase. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Hormuz commercial transit data through September confirmed the F4 threshold of 30 vessels per day as unachievable by the October 3 horizon. IMF PortWatch recorded approximately 3.17 vessels per day in early September; mid-month readings reached 7–14 on better days before falling again; live tracking cited 4 vessels on September 30. Three confirmed indicators remain NOT_OBSERVED: no named P&amp;I insurer reinstated war-risk coverage; no sustained 72-hour halt in IRGC mine/vessel-attack activity occurred — CENTCOM struck Iranian mine-laying assets on a Hormuz island in late September, confirming active IRGC operations; no mediator-confirmed Iranian commitment to halt mine deployment was announced. The 30-vessel 7-day rolling average cannot be reached from a base of 4–14 vessels in the 48 hours before the October 3 cutoff. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Israeli election polling through September 29 showed the race closer than the September 3 board anticipated, with Yashar, not Likud, holding the single-party lead in four of five surveys. The September 9 i24NEWS poll (Likud 27, Yashar 22) was the sole outlier; the September 23 Channel 13 survey put Yashar at 22 and Likud at 18; the September 29 Kan News poll placed blocs at 52–52 with Yashar at 23 seats and Likud at 21. No formal Likud pre-election alliance with right-bloc partners was announced. The wartime security context did not produce the wartime incumbency boost the September 3 board's 0.42 estimate incorporated; one Channel 13 survey showed 48% of respondents preferring Eisenkot as prime minister compared to 36% for Netanyahu. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Trump publicly rejected Iran's September 24 seven-day road map on September 27, characterizing it as an "offer of desperation," while stating that indirect talks would continue. Araghchi told NBC News the formal rejection had not yet arrived through mediators; Tehran announced September 30 it had received the US counterproposal through Qatar without disclosing its contents. Government spokeswoman Mohajerani described the document as "under review." Trump's comment that a deal was unlikely before the November 3 US midterm elections establishes the domestic electoral constraint on the US side, while Iran's dual-track posture — "fully prepared for doomsday war" and "diplomacy not over" simultaneously — represents the deliberate strategic ambiguity that has characterized each negotiating phase since April. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

