      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Further acknowledged strikes in the 7-day window (N2, 60%) alongside deadlock on Hormuz-sanctions-ceasefire sequencing could extend the kinetic exchange beyond the Strait zone to broader Iranian-held positions.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Written-proposal exchange continues through Qatar/Pakistan with no breakthrough; Hormuz remains at approximately 5–15% of prewar volume; the November 3 US midterm constraint holds both sides in a holding pattern through early November.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Iran transmits written response this week (N1, 55%) and the Qatari shuttle advances toward a ceasefire framework agreement by November 15 (N4, 22%) in the post-midterm window after November 4.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media coverage of the September 23–30 period treated Araghchi's UNGA encounter with US envoys as a demonstration of the Islamic Republic's diplomatic initiative rather than a concession to American pressure. IRNA and Press TV described Iran's September 24 written road map as a "comprehensive and generous framework," attributing the framing to good faith under sustained aggression, with Trump's September 27 rejection cast as a domestically calculated decision driven by November midterm electoral considerations rather than a genuine security assessment. IRIB gave extended airtime to commentators arguing that American inability to deliver Hormuz normalization without Iranian consent constituted evidence of US strategic failure in the seventh month of the conflict. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Coverage of the Hormuz situation on state channels described the Strait's operational status as a legitimate sovereign instrument, not a humanitarian or economic liability. The domestic consequences of the closure — fuel import disruptions, currency volatility, petrochemical sector strain — received no significant coverage on IRNA, IRIB, or Tasnim; reformist and semi-independent outlets carried limited economic reporting but were not elevated in state media packages. Iran's "southern export corridors" moving crude through alternative routes received favorable framing as proof of economic resilience and operational adaptability under siege conditions. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Araghchi's September 27 warning that Iran was "fully prepared for doomsday war" received prominent placement in Kayhan and Iran newspapers and extensive IRIB broadcast time, alongside footage of late September pro-government rallies in Tehran, Isfahan, and Mashhad framed as popular endorsement of the IRGC's resistance doctrine. The Supreme Leader's office issued no direct public statement on the US counterproposal received September 30, consistent with the deliberate public ambiguity posture observed during both the April and June negotiating rounds — maintaining plausible deniability on content and position until internal review is complete. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

